---
title: Korma Dal with Honey-Lime Halloumi
description: Creamy, gently spiced Dal topped with golden, sticky honey-lime Halloumi, toasted almonds and fresh coriander.
servings: 4
source: https://www.mob.co.uk/recipes/korma-dal-with-honey-halloumi
---

-- Prep lentils & aromatics

Rinse @red lentils{300%g} under cold water until the water runs clear. Finely dice @onion{1}, finely grate the @garlic cloves{3} and @fresh ginger{2%tsp}. Separate the leaves and stems from @fresh coriander{15%g} and finely chop the stems.

-- Build the flavour base

Heat a #large saucepan{} over a medium heat with @olive oil{1%tbsp}. Add the onion with a pinch of @salt{} and cook for ~{6-8%minutes} until soft and starting to turn golden.

Add the garlic, ginger and chopped coriander stems and cook for ~{1%minute}.

-- Fry the paste

Add @korma curry paste{3%tbsp} and @tomato purée{1%tbsp}. Fry for ~{2%minutes}, stirring regularly, until fragrant and the tomato purée has darkened slightly.

-- Simmer the dal

Stir in the lentils, @vegetable stock{300%ml} and @coconut milk{400%ml}. Bring to the boil, then turn the heat down and simmer uncovered for ~{18-20%minutes}, stirring occasionally, until the lentils are completely soft and the dal is thick and creamy. Add a splash of @water{} if it becomes too thick before the lentils are cooked.

Season with @ground black pepper{} and @salt{} to taste, bearing in mind the halloumi and stock are already salty.

-- Toast the almonds

Place @flaked almonds{30%g} in a dry #frying pan{} over a medium heat and toast for ~{2-3%minutes} until lightly golden. Tip onto a plate and set aside.

-- Fry the halloumi

Cut @halloumi{225%g} into 2cm chunks. Return the frying pan to a medium-high heat. Add the halloumi and cook without oil for ~{4-5%minutes}, turning until deeply golden on all sides.

-- Glaze with honey & lime

Reduce the heat to low. Add @honey{2%tbsp} and @lime juice{2%tsp} and toss quickly until the halloumi is glossy and sticky. Remove from the heat before the honey catches.

-- Finish the dal

Stir @lime juice{0.5%lime} through the dal. Taste and adjust the salt, pepper and lime as needed.

-- Serve

Spoon the dal into bowls and top with the sticky honey-lime halloumi. Finish with the coriander leaves, toasted almonds and a pinch of @chilli flakes{}.

Serve as-is or bulk out with rice or naan.

-- Notes: Use a full 400ml tin of coconut milk for convenience. Halloumi is typically sold in 225g blocks, so one block works perfectly here. If your vegetable stock is particularly salty, use low-salt stock or make it slightly weaker than usual. The lime balances the richness of the coconut milk and halloumi, so add a little more to taste if needed. Leftovers keep well in the fridge for up to 3 days; the dal thickens as it sits, so add a splash of water when reheating. Keep leftover halloumi separate where possible and reheat it in a frying pan.